target

# Python bindings build artifacts.
#
# `_magical_rs.pyi` is deliberately NOT ignored: it is a hand-written type
# stub, and `bindings/python/tests/test_stub.py` checks it against the
# compiled module. The compiled module beside it is an artifact.
*.pyd
*.so
*.pdb
bindings/python/dist/
bindings/python/.venv/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/

# NodeJS bindings build artifacts.
#
# `bindings/nodejs/index.d.ts` and `bindings/nodejs/_kinds.d.ts` are deliberately
# NOT ignored. The first is a hand-written type declaration, and `test/types.ts`
# checks its generics against real call sites; the second is generated but
# checked in, because `test/kinds.test.js` compares it against the compiled
# module's own discriminants, and a generated file nobody can diff is a generated
# file nobody can review. The `.wasm` beside them is an artifact of the build.
bindings/nodejs/magical_js.wasm
bindings/nodejs/node_modules/
# `nodejs_publish.yml` packs into `dist/` and a contributor reproducing the release
# locally does the same. `bindings/python/dist/` is ignored for the same reason.
bindings/nodejs/dist/

# An `.npmrc` anywhere in this repository is ignored, and that is a security rule
# rather than a tidiness one: a project-local `.npmrc` is where an auth token lands
# when somebody runs `npm login --location=project` or sets one by hand, and npm
# does not warn about committing it. `npm login` with no flag writes to the *user*
# config outside this tree, so nothing legitimate is ignored by this line -- and the
# one that would be is the one that must not be committed. Credentials belong on the
# machine, and `nodejs_publish.yml` gets its token from CI's own secret store.
.npmrc

# Planning notes are working documents, not project documentation, so they do
# not belong here. They are written to the agent's scratch directory instead.
# `PLAN.md` has no leading slash, so this matches the name at any depth.
PLAN.md
